A yellow circle, oversized black lettering, cobalt blue, and a red diagonal. The starting point for this redesign is the [Japanese cover of *Sonic the Hedgehog* for Mega Drive](https://vgdensetsu.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Akira-Watanabe-Sonic-the-hedgehog-JPN.jpg), reproduced in [VGDensetsu’s illustration archive](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/). The typography and geometry do a remarkable amount of work around the character illustration.

That is the part I wanted to bring into this site: a cover with some energy, followed by somewhere comfortable to read.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - The direct reference is Japanese Sonic packaging: flat colour, compressed lettering, tilted labels, and a few geometric marks.
> - The homepage puts a piece of actual work beside the introduction, then gives recent writing its own blue section.
> - The article layout borrows the palette and display type while keeping the paragraphs upright and quiet.
> - The same visual system now carries through the shared layouts, indexes, profile, search and style reference.

## From a datasheet to a cover

The earlier direction leaned into the site as a technical document: graph paper, numbered sections, small labels, and rules around most things. That suited the systems side of my work, but the accumulation made the homepage feel more like a register than an introduction.

I tried opening it up first. Removing the frames helped, although the shared grid introduced an awkward step: the headline started at the page edge, and the biography jumped into the column reserved for article titles. The alignment made sense in the stylesheet and looked awkward on the page.

The cartridge reference gave the introduction a different job. It could be a cover: one large statement, a clear visual identity, and a project worth opening. The writing list could then take over as an index.

My background includes newspaper graphics and print production, alongside the platform and information-architecture work. Large type, a strong colour separation, and the relationship between a cover and its contents feel like useful material to return to here.

