Japanese Mega Drive packaging, reproduced here as design references. Artwork and branding © SEGA. See the [VGDensetsu archive for the covers and its illustration and packaging credits](https://vgdensetsu.net/megadrivegallerys3/); individual design credits have not been independently verified here.

The yellow disc gives the headline a field to sit on. The black and cobalt lines have different weights of attention even though they share a typeface. A black ribbon carries the short description of the practice: “Platforms. Content. People. Connected by design.” The red diagonal makes room for the featured project as a tilted blue plate.

A small cluster of angular marks completes the composition. The same vocabulary appears selectively in the margins of code blocks and quotations: a serrated edge, a complete squiggle or a yellow accent. The marks have their own space, leaving the text clear.

The period research also led to [Ettore Sottsass’s drawings for *Casablanca* and *Carlton*, reproduced by the V&A](https://www.vam.ac.uk/info/collection-selection-boxes-postmodern-design), and [April Greiman’s work and interview in *Eye on Design*](https://eyeondesign.aiga.org/april-greiman-is-still-ahead-of-the-curve/). The angles, scale changes, and willingness to treat lettering as an image are useful comparisons. They help describe the territory around the reference; I’m not claiming a documented line of influence from those works to Sonic’s packaging.

The site’s marks are newly drawn SVG geometry. The character illustration and SEGA branding stay with the source artwork.

## A second reference: fragments on paper

A [TurboGrafx-16 print advertisement](https://archives.tg-16.com/EGM_1989_07.htm) brought another idea into the exploration. Its face and game imagery break into unequal shards across a wide white field. The heavy black shapes anchor the composition; small red and yellow overlaps give the fragments different depths. The empty space between the pieces does as much work as the pieces themselves.

