## Give the introduction something to point at

“Make it make sense” is the cover line. The next task is to show what that means in practice.

The featured plate links to [the UNDRR work connecting Storybook, structured content, design tokens, and Figma](/work/2026/impact-story-mangrove-figma/). Its title, image, and description come from the published entry. That puts evidence beside the introduction, where someone arriving for the first time can find it.

Below that, “Ideas in circulation” contains six recent entries from the live collection, with their real dates, teasers, and thumbnails. The masthead offers direct routes to writing, work, my profile, and search. The visual treatment is more assertive, but the navigation still has ordinary jobs to do.

## Let the article settle down

The [article layout](/posts/20261024-when-one-person-leaves-a-team-leaves/) carries over the near-white paper ground, yellow disc, and condensed title. Diagonal colour boundaries, small sawtooth edges and sparse geometric marks draw from the packaging’s print details; the paragraphs stay on plain paper. The reading column uses IBM Plex, upright paragraphs, and restrained cobalt headings. The contents are a native HTML disclosure, so readers can open the outline without another script.

The cover line is now trying [Science Gothic](https://github.com/googlefonts/science-gothic), whose square forms bring a more technical voice to the packaging direction. The homepage uses a small fixed instance at weight 750, width 60, slant −5 and zero stroke contrast. The headline’s baseline stays level: the letterforms already lean, leaving the ribbon and project plate to carry the rotation. Only the small cover instance is shipped. [Anton](https://github.com/google/fonts/tree/main/ofl/anton) remains on the other display headings and social cards. Both are self-hosted with their SIL Open Font Licenses. Plex Sans remains the body and interface face. For code and supporting labels, I’m trying [League Mono](https://github.com/theleagueof/league-mono), a self-hosted variable font with weight and width axes. Code and metadata use weight 400 at normal width.

The circle and diagonal are CSS shapes; small SVG assets supply the margin marks and serrated-edge mask. The basic layout and graphic edges use no JavaScript. The optional collage figure uses a small pointer handler for parallax, moving its CSS layers gently at different depths. That enhancement stays static on touch devices and when reduced motion is requested. The existing CSS toggle still removes the styling, and the content remains in document order. Those constraints are useful: the graphic decisions have to work with the page’s structure.

## Still a working direction

This implementation is collected in [PR #168](https://github.com/khawkins98/allaboutken-11ty/pull/168). The [proposal board](/design-lab/megadrive/) keeps the earlier options available for comparison. The shared layouts now carry the system through articles, notes, photographs, interactive stories, indexes, profile and utility pages. The [design guide](/style-guide/design/) records the principles and the [component reference](/style-guide/components/) shows the live patterns.

The question I want to keep testing is whether the page gives a clearer sense of the person and the work, while staying easy to read. The cover can be loud. The paragraphs have to earn the next few minutes.

